.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-DELETE-EGRESS-SITE 1 "November 2026" "euclid-cli 2.1.3" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-delete-egress-site \- forget one outside site of an application
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap delete-egress-site
.BR \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
.BR \-\-site | \-s
.I HOST
.SH DESCRIPTION
Forgets one outside site of one application entirely: its limit, its
token bucket, its concurrency slots and when it was last called. Where
.BR euclid-cli-eap-delete-egress-limit (1)
takes back a limit and leaves the site listed, this leaves nothing.
.PP
It is not a ban. A site is listed because the application asked for a
permit to call it, so a site it still calls is listed again on its next
request, under whatever limit it inherits. Use this for a site that is
finished with: a decommissioned host, or a limit set on a misspelled
name.
.PP
Anything in flight for the site loses its slots at once, since the
permits were counted in the state this removes. The release that follows
finds nothing and does nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-application\-id " " \fINAME\fR
The application the site belongs to. Required.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-site " " \fIHOST\fR
Host name of the outside site, or
.B *
for the application's default. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The site was removed.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, the server could not be reached, or the
caller may not change the application.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap list-egress-sites --application-id onix-parser
euclid-cli eap delete-egress-site --application-id onix-parser \e
    --site api.old-supplier.example
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-list-egress-sites (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-set-egress-limit (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-delete-egress-limit (1)
